      <p class="lead">Yes, a vibe-coded app can get its owner sued or fined, and usually not because anyone hacked it. <strong>Lawsuits and regulators target ordinary features: a tracking script that runs before consent, a session recorder that captures what users type, a promo text nobody agreed to, or a subscription that is hard to cancel.</strong> AI coding tools build those features quickly and without legal context. Best Answer Hub wrote this playbook to separate the real risks from the inflated ones, using the statutes, court rulings, and regulator decisions behind each viral claim.</p>

      <p>This playbook has a companion on accessibility, <a href="/playbooks/ada-website-lawsuits-vibe-coded-apps/">ADA Website Lawsuits: What Vibe-Coded Apps Get Wrong</a>, which covers the largest single source of website lawsuits. Both feed one free instrument: the Best Answer Hub <a href="/knowledge-center/vibe-coder/">Vibe Coder Readiness Check</a>, whose compliance dimension scores privacy, consent, and accessibility law.</p>

      <div class="callout peach"><div class="h">Read this first</div><p>This is plain-English education, not legal advice. Best Answer Hub is not a law firm, the rules differ by country and state, and several of them changed during 2025 and 2026. If you have received a demand letter, a regulator inquiry, or a lawsuit, speak to a lawyer before you reply, pay, or change anything, because what you do next can matter.</p></div>

      <div class="cav">Start with responsibility</div>
      <h2 id="liable">Who is liable when an AI wrote the code?</h2>
      <p>The business that runs the app, every time. Each law in this playbook assigns its duties to the operator, seller, or sender: COPPA speaks of the "operator" of a website, CAN-SPAM of the "sender," the federal subscription law of the person who charges the card, and GDPR of the "controller" who decides why data is processed. None of them excuses a founder because a tool wrote the feature. The US Federal Trade Commission said it directly when it launched Operation AI Comply in September 2024: "there is no AI exemption from the laws on the books."</p>
      <p>The same logic applies when something goes wrong. Under GDPR Article 33, a personal data breach must be reported to the regulator without undue delay and, where feasible, within 72 hours of the controller becoming aware of it. That duty sits with the app owner, not the AI builder. Best Answer Hub's practical reading: treat every line an agent ships as a line you wrote, because legally it is.</p>

      <div class="cav">The fact-check</div>
      <h2 id="viral">Are the viral "your app can get sued" claims true?</h2>
      <p>About half hold up as stated, and the rest either inflate the money or misstate the rule. Two widely shared short videos list the same family of risks: Google Fonts, keystroke recording, under-13 sign-ups, launch emails, promo texts, and subscription checkouts. The table checks each claim against the primary source.</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>The viral claim</th><th>Verdict</th><th>What the record shows</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="band">Google Fonts can get you sued "for hundreds of thousands"</td><td><span class="score lv3">Inflated</span></td><td>One German court awarded €100. The €250,000 figure is the maximum fine for ignoring that court's order.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="band">Keystroke recording costs "$5,000 per session" in California</td><td><span class="score lv2">Overstated</span></td><td>$5,000 per violation in statutory damages, which a plaintiff must win in court. Many session replay suits fail.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="band">An under-13 user can cost "$53,000"</td><td><span class="score lv1">Close</span></td><td>Up to $53,088 per COPPA violation, but only for child-directed services or actual knowledge.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="band">Your signup form must verify age</td><td><span class="score lv3">Wrong</span></td><td>The FTC says COPPA does not require operators to ask visitors' ages.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="band">Launch emails need a postal address, or "$53,000 per email"</td><td><span class="score lv1">True</span></td><td>CAN-SPAM requires a valid postal address; up to $53,088 per violating email.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="band">Emails legally need one-click unsubscribe</td><td><span class="score lv2">Misattributed</span></td><td>CAN-SPAM requires a working opt-out. One-click is a Gmail bulk-sender rule, not a statute.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="band">Promo texts without consent cost "$500 per text"</td><td><span class="score lv1">True</span></td><td>$500 per message under the TCPA, up to $1,500 if willful.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="band">Renewal terms must sit next to the purchase button</td><td><span class="score lv1">Mostly true</span></td><td>California requires them "in visual proximity" to the consent request, before purchase.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="band">User uploads "must have the correct disclaimer"</td><td><span class="score lv2">Too vague</span></td><td>No single upload disclaimer law exists; the real duties depend on what users upload.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>The pattern matters more than any single row. The videos are right that ordinary features carry legal duties, and wrong whenever they turn a maximum into a typical bill or a deliverability rule into a statute. Best Answer Hub's view is that founders act better on accurate numbers than on frightening ones, so every figure below links to its source.</p>

      <div class="cav">The famous one</div>
      <h2 id="fonts">Can Google Fonts really get my app sued in Europe?</h2>
      <p>It happened once, for €100, and the copycat wave that followed became a criminal investigation. On 20 January 2022 the Munich Regional Court (LG München I, case 3 O 17493/20) ruled that a website broke GDPR by loading Google Fonts from Google's servers, which sent a visitor's IP address to Google in the United States without a legal basis. The court ordered the operator to stop and awarded the visitor €100 in damages for his loss of control over the data. It rejected the site's "legitimate interest" defense partly because the fonts could have been used without contacting Google at all, which is to say self-hosted.</p>
      <p>The "hundreds of thousands" in the viral version most likely comes from the injunction itself: a breach of the court's order risks a penalty of up to €250,000. That is a cap on punishment for defying a court, not damages for using a font. Later in 2022, one Berlin lawyer sent mass letters demanding €170 from site owners, and Berlin prosecutors opened an investigation for attempted extortion and fraud covering at least 2,418 cases, with up to €346,000 in assets frozen. German courts in 2023 then rejected similar claims as abusive, including a second Munich ruling that found the claimant had deliberately provoked the visits for profit.</p>
      <div class="callout sage"><div class="h">The fix costs nothing</div><p>Self-host your fonts. Download the font files, serve them from your own domain, and delete every link to fonts.googleapis.com and fonts.gstatic.com. Pages load slightly faster, visitors' browsers stop contacting a third party, and the whole question disappears. Best Answer Hub moved its own fonts in-house in November 2026 for exactly this reason. Tell your agent: "Self-host all web fonts; the app must make no requests to Google Fonts."</p></div>
      <p>The bigger European risk is not fonts but tracking without consent. Under the ePrivacy Directive, non-essential cookies and trackers need consent first, and regulators enforce it hard: France's CNIL fined SHEIN €150 million in September 2025 because trackers ran before visitors touched the cookie banner and kept running after "reject all," and fined the publisher of vanityfair.fr €750,000 that November for 38 cookies that survived a refusal. In 2025 the CNIL issued 83 sanctions totalling about €486.8 million. GDPR reaches beyond Europe too: it covers non-EU businesses that offer services to people in the EU or monitor their behavior there, and European regulators count tracking cookies as monitoring. The Dutch regulator once fined a non-EU website €525,000 for failing to appoint an EU representative, which shows the reach is real even if such cases are rare.</p>

      <div class="cav">The quiet recorder</div>
      <h2 id="tracking">Does session replay or tracking break California law?</h2>
      <p>It can, and California is where the lawsuits concentrate. The California Invasion of Privacy Act (CIPA) lets a plaintiff recover the greater of $5,000 per violation or three times actual damages, and actual harm need not be proven. Since 2022, plaintiffs have used its wiretap section to sue websites over session replay tools that record clicks and keystrokes, advertising pixels from Meta and TikTok, and chat widgets. Court filings understate the activity, because many of these claims settle at the demand letter stage or go to arbitration.</p>
      <p>The outcomes are genuinely mixed, which is why "$5,000 per session" overstates it. In Javier v. Assurance IQ (2022), a federal appeals court said consent must come before recording starts, so a privacy policy accepted afterwards was too late. In Popa v. Microsoft (August 2025), the same court dismissed a session replay case over Microsoft Clarity because nothing embarrassing or private had been captured. California's SB 690, signed on 30 September 2026 and operative from 1 January 2027, now lets only the Attorney General bring the "pen register" claims built on IP address collection, but it leaves private wiretap suits over session replay, pixels, and chat untouched.</p>
