        <p class="chart-src">Sources: FTC COPPA FAQ and CAN-SPAM guide (2025 levels kept for 2026); Cal. Penal Code 637.2; 47 USC 227. COPPA and CAN-SPAM figures are maximum civil penalties; CIPA and TCPA figures are statutory damages a plaintiff must win. Each multiplies by the number of violations.</p>
      </figure>
      <p>The last line of that caption is the real lesson. The scary numbers are rarely about one big fine; they are small amounts multiplied by every user, email, text, or recorded session. That is why defaults matter so much in a vibe-coded app: one default, repeated across thousands of visits, is how a $500 rule becomes a serious bill. Best Answer Hub recommends a simple standing rule for any agent: nothing that records, tracks, or shares visitor data loads before the visitor says yes.</p>

      <div class="cav">The under-13 question</div>
      <h2 id="kids">Do I need age verification for COPPA?</h2>
      <p>No, and that is where the viral advice goes wrong. The FTC's own COPPA guidance says the law "does not require operators to ask the age of visitors." COPPA applies in two situations: when a website or app is directed to children under 13, or when a general-audience operator has actual knowledge that it is collecting personal information from a child under 13. In either case the duties are real: clear notice, verifiable parental consent before collecting a child's data, and, under the amended COPPA Rule that took effect in June 2025 with compliance required by 22 April 2026, a written data security program and separate parental consent before sharing children's data with third parties.</p>
      <p>The penalty figure in the video is close to right. The FTC can seek up to $53,088 per violation, and it kept its 2025 penalty levels for 2026 after that year's inflation adjustment was cancelled. If children might plausibly use your app, a neutral age screen helps: ask for a date of birth without hinting at the "right" answer, and a general-audience app may then block users under 13. A service aimed even partly at children may not block them altogether and must handle parental consent instead. Best Answer Hub suggests deciding which of those three positions your app is in before launch, and writing it down.</p>

      <div class="cav">Inbox and phone</div>
      <h2 id="messages">What do launch emails and promo texts legally need?</h2>
      <p>Emails need honesty, an address, and a working way out; texts need written consent first. Under the CAN-SPAM Act, a commercial email must not use misleading headers or subject lines, must identify itself as an ad where applicable, and must include a valid physical postal address, which can be a registered PO box or private mailbox. It needs an opt-out that keeps working for at least 30 days after sending, and opt-outs must be honored within 10 business days. Each violating email can cost up to $53,088. Receipts and other transactional emails are exempt from most of these rules.</p>
      <p>One-click unsubscribe is not in CAN-SPAM. It is a Gmail requirement, in force since 1 February 2024, for anyone sending more than 5,000 messages a day, using the standard List-Unsubscribe header. Best Answer Hub still recommends it for every launch list, because the inbox providers that enforce it decide whether your launch email arrives at all.</p>
      <p>Texts are stricter. The Telephone Consumer Protection Act allows $500 per unlawful message, up to $1,500 if willful, and automated marketing texts need prior express written consent: a signed agreement, which can be electronic, that clearly authorizes the messages and states that agreeing is not a condition of purchase. Roughly 2,800 TCPA lawsuits were filed in 2025. An FCC order adopted in September 2026 also requires opt-out requests to be honored within a reasonable time, no more than 10 business days. Tell your agent: "No marketing text or email goes out unless a stored consent record exists for that user, with the date and the wording they agreed to."</p>

      <div class="cav">At the checkout</div>
      <h2 id="checkout">What must a subscription checkout show?</h2>
      <p>The renewal terms, clearly, before the customer pays, plus a separate yes and an easy exit. The federal Restore Online Shoppers' Confidence Act requires online sellers with a subscription or free trial to disclose all material terms clearly and conspicuously before taking billing details, to get the customer's express informed consent before charging, and to provide a simple way to stop recurring charges. The FTC's broader "click-to-cancel" rule is not in force: a federal appeals court vacated it on 8 July 2025, and the FTC restarted rulemaking in 2026 without a final rule as of this writing.</p>
      <p>California sets the practical standard, and its amended Automatic Renewal Law has applied since 1 July 2025. Renewal terms must appear clearly and conspicuously before the subscription starts and "in visual proximity" to the request for consent, which is why the viral advice to put them right next to the purchase button is close to right. The customer must give express affirmative consent to the renewal terms themselves, receive an acknowledgment they can keep, get an annual reminder on annual plans, and be able to cancel online with a prominent direct link or button. A retention offer is allowed only if a click-to-cancel button sits alongside it.</p>
      <div class="callout"><div class="h">A payment provider does not make your page compliant</div><p>Stripe, Paddle, and other providers process the charge, but the pricing page, the renewal wording, the consent checkbox, and the cancel flow are usually your screens. Best Answer Hub recommends a one-line test: could a stranger read your checkout and say, before paying, exactly what they will be charged, how often, and how to stop it? If not, the agent has more work to do.</p></div>

      <div class="cav">Do these today</div>
      <h2 id="week">What should I fix in my app this week?</h2>
      <p>Six standing rules close the gaps the viral videos point at, and each one is a sentence your agent can act on. Paste them into the project rules file, such as CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md, so they bind every future session, then ask the agent to show evidence for each.</p>
      <div class="sec">
        <ul class="pillars">
          <li><span class="dot">1</span><div><b>Self-host fonts and assets.</b> "The app makes no requests to Google Fonts or other font CDNs." Passing looks like zero third-party font requests in the browser's network tab.</div></li>
          <li><span class="dot">2</span><div><b>Consent before any tracker.</b> "No analytics, ads, pixel, chat, or replay script loads until the visitor accepts, and rejecting is as easy as accepting." Passing looks like zero third-party requests on a fresh visit before you click anything.</div></li>
          <li><span class="dot">3</span><div><b>Never record what users type.</b> "Session replay, if used at all, masks every input field." Passing looks like masked fields in a test recording.</div></li>
          <li><span class="dot">4</span><div><b>Decide your COPPA position.</b> General audience, mixed, or child-directed, written down, with a neutral age screen if children might sign up.</div></li>
          <li><span class="dot">5</span><div><b>Consent records before messages.</b> "No marketing email or text is sent without a stored consent record; every email carries a postal address and a working unsubscribe link." Passing looks like a consent row behind every subscriber.</div></li>
          <li><span class="dot">6</span><div><b>An honest checkout.</b> "Renewal price, frequency, and how to cancel appear beside the subscribe button; renewal terms get their own consent; cancel works online in a few clicks." Passing looks like a stranger explaining the terms back to you.</div></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <p>Those six turn a scary list into evidence you produced yourself. Accessibility is the remaining big exposure, covered step by step in the companion <a href="/playbooks/ada-website-lawsuits-vibe-coded-apps/">ADA website lawsuit playbook</a>, and the security side of launching lives in <a href="/playbooks/vibe-coded-app-launch-readiness/">Is Your Vibe-Coded App Ready to Launch?</a></p>

      <div class="cta">
        <span class="kicker">Accurate beats alarming.</span>
        <h3>Take the free Vibe Coder Readiness Check</h3>
        <p>45 questions across nine dimensions, including privacy, consent, and accessibility law, with a 0 to 100 score in about 10 to 12 minutes. No signup and no email, and the score is never paywalled.</p>
        <a class="btn" href="/knowledge-center/vibe-coder/">Score your app now</a>
      </div>

      <div class="cav">Good questions</div>
      <h2 id="faq">Common questions about vibe-coded apps and the law</h2>
      <div class="faq">
          {faqs.map((f) => (
            <div class="qa">
              <div class="q" set:html={f.q}></div>
              <div class="a" set:html={f.a}></div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

      <div class="cav" style="margin-top:38px">For the record</div>
      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ol class="sources">
        <li>US Federal Trade Commission, <a href="https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2024/09/ftc-announces-crackdown-deceptive-ai-claims-schemes" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FTC announces crackdown on deceptive AI claims and schemes</a>, September 2024 ("no AI exemption from the laws on the books").</li>
        <li><a href="https://gdpr-info.eu/art-33-gdpr/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GDPR Article 33</a> (72-hour breach notification by the controller) and <a href="https://gdpr-info.eu/art-3-gdpr/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Article 3</a> (territorial scope).</li>
        <li>Landgericht München I, <a href="https://www.gesetze-bayern.de/Content/Document/Y-300-Z-BECKRS-B-2022-N-612" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">judgment of 20 January 2022, 3 O 17493/20</a> (Google Fonts; €100 damages; injunction with penalty up to €250,000).</li>
        <li>heise online, <a href="https://www.heise.de/news/Google-Fonts-Durchsuchungen-wegen-Abmahnbetrugs-vermeintlicher-Datenschuetzer-7440620.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google Fonts: searches over warning-letter fraud</a>, December 2022 (Berlin prosecutors; at least 2,418 cases; up to €346,000 frozen).</li>
        <li>Händlerbund, <a href="https://ohn.haendlerbund.de/recht/urteile-entscheidungen/138187-urteil-zu-google-fonts-abmahnungen-ohrfeige-fuer-massenabmahner" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LG München I, 30 March 2023, 4 O 13063/22</a> (mass Google Fonts claims rejected).</li>
        <li>CNIL, <a href="https://www.cnil.fr/fr/cookies-deposes-sans-consentement-la-cnil-sanctionne-shein-dune-amende-de-150-millions-deuros" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cookies placed without consent: SHEIN fined €150 million</a>, September 2025.</li>
        <li>CNIL, <a href="https://www.cnil.fr/en/cookies-placed-without-consent-company-publishes-website-vanityfairfr-fined-750000-euros" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">vanityfair.fr publisher fined €750,000</a>, November 2025.</li>
        <li>CNIL, <a href="https://cnil.fr/en/node/168166" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">2025 sanctions review</a>, February 2026 (83 sanctions, about €486.8 million).</li>
        <li>European Data Protection Board, <a href="https://www.edpb.europa.eu/our-work-tools/our-documents/guidelines/guidelines-32018-territorial-scope-gdpr-article-3-version_en" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Guidelines 3/2018 on the territorial scope of the GDPR</a> (tracking cookies as monitoring).</li>
        <li>Hunton Andrews Kurth, <a href="https://www.hunton.com/privacy-and-cybersecurity-law-blog/dutch-dpa-fines-company-525000-eur-for-failure-to-designate-eu-representative" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Dutch DPA fines company €525,000 for failing to designate an EU representative</a>, 2021.</li>
        <li>legislation.gov.uk, <a href="https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2026/82/regulation/2/made" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Data (Use and Access) Act 2025 commencement</a> (cookie changes in force 5 February 2026).</li>
        <li>California Penal Code section 637.2, <a href="https://california.public.law/codes/ca_penal_code_section_637.2" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">civil action and $5,000 per violation</a>.</li>
        <li>Eric Goldman, <a href="https://blog.ericgoldman.org/archives/2025/08/ninth-circuit-dismisses-session-replay-lawsuit-popa-v-microsoft.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ninth Circuit dismisses session replay lawsuit, Popa v. Microsoft</a>, August 2025.</li>
        <li>Sidley Austin, <a href="https://www.sidley.com/en/insights/newsupdates/2026/09/californias-sb-690-clears-the-legislature-what-it-means-for-cipa-website-tracking-claims" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">California's SB 690 and CIPA website tracking claims</a>, September 2026.</li>
        <li>Jackson Walker, <a href="https://www.jw.com/news/insights-california-invasion-privacy-act-claims-surge/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">California Invasion of Privacy Act claims surge</a>, December 2025 (session replay, pixels, chat widgets).</li>
        <li>US Federal Trade Commission, <a href="https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/complying-coppa-frequently-asked-questions" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Complying with COPPA: Frequently Asked Questions</a> (up to $53,088 per violation; no duty to ask visitors' ages; neutral age screens).</li>
        <li>Federal Register, <a href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-04-22/html/2025-05904.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Children's Online Privacy Protection Rule, final amendments</a>, 22 April 2025 (effective 23 June 2025; compliance by 22 April 2026).</li>
        <li>Federal Register, <a href="https://public-inspection.federalregister.gov/2026-18853.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FTC civil penalty amounts unchanged for 2026</a>, September 2026.</li>
